.TH LOCAL-BACKUP 1 "2026-11-14" "OTPme 0.3.0a288" "OTPme Manual"
.SH NAME
local-backup \- work on a backup repository directly
.SH SYNOPSIS
.na
.nh
.B local-backup
\fB\-d\fR \fIrepository\fR
[\fB\-k\fR \fIkey\fR | \fB\-p\fR \fIpassword_file\fR]
[\fB\-v\fR]
[\fB\-\-no\-compress\fR]
.I command
[\fIarguments\fR]
.hy
.ad
.SH DESCRIPTION
.na
.nh
.B local-backup
works on a repository in a directory of this machine, without a backup server and without a realm.
It is the tool for the backup server itself: check a repository, free space, or apply the retention by hand.
It can also back up into a directory and restore from it, on any machine.
.PP
\fIrepository\fR is the directory of the repository.
On a backup server of OTPme that is \fI<BACKUP_DIR>/<site>/<type>/<name>\fR, for example \fI/var/backups/berlin/share/data\fR, see
.BR otpme\-backup (1).
Only \fBbackup\fR makes a repository; every other command on a directory without one says so and changes nothing.
.PP
A command that reads or writes the files needs the key: \fBbackup\fR, \fBrestore\fR, \fBls\fR and \fBverify\fR.
\fB\-k\fR takes it as 64 hex characters; for a repository of OTPme that is the \fBbackup_key\fR of the object.
\fB\-p\fR takes a file with a password instead, from which a key is made; without either of them it is asked for.
A repository of OTPme is encrypted with the key, so a password does not open it.
The other commands work on the repository alone and need neither.
.PP
The commands that change the repository, and \fBverify\fR, take the lock of the repository, the one a backup, a restore and a repack of the backup daemon take as well.
.hy
.ad
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-d\fR, \fB\-\-backup\-dir \fIrepository
The repository to work on.
.TP
.B \-k\fR, \fB\-\-key \fIkey
The key, 64 hex characters.
.TP
.B \-p\fR, \fB\-\-password\-file \fIfile
A file with the password a key is made from.
.TP
.B \-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose
Say more.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-compress
For \fBbackup\fR: store the files without compressing them first.
.SH COMMANDS
.na
.nh
.TP
\fBbackup\fR [\fB\-\-name\fR \fIname\fR] [\fB\-\-special\-files\fR] [\fB\-\-exclude\fR \fIpattern\fR] [\fB\-\-include\fR \fIpattern\fR] [\fB\-\-mode\fR \fBtree\fR|\fBpack\fR] [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] \fIsource\fR
Back up the directory \fIsource\fR into a new snapshot, named after the time it started unless \fB\-\-name\fR says otherwise.
The patterns work as with \fBotpme\-backup start\fR.
Device files, FIFOs and sockets are left out unless \fB\-\-special\-files\fR is given.
\fB\-\-mode\fR is taken by a new repository only (default: pack); an existing one keeps its mode.
.TP
\fBrestore\fR [\fB\-\-path\fR \fIpath\fR] [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] \fIsnapshot\fR \fIdestination\fR
Restore a snapshot into \fIdestination\fR, or with \fB\-\-path\fR only that file or directory.
.TP
.B list
List the snapshots, as \fBotpme\-backup list\fR does.
.TP
\fBls\fR [\fB\-\-full\-path\fR] [\fB\-R\fR] \fIsnapshot\fR [\fIpath\fR]
List what a snapshot holds, as \fBotpme\-backup ls\fR does.
.TP
.B verify \fIsnapshot\fR
Read every block of the snapshot and check that it is the block it should be.
The exit code is 1 when one is not.
.TP
.B delete \fIsnapshot\fR
Delete the snapshot, and the blocks no other snapshot uses.
.TP
\fBretention\fR [\fB\-\-daily\fR \fIn\fR] [\fB\-\-weekly\fR \fIn\fR] [\fB\-\-monthly\fR \fIn\fR] [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR]
Apply the retention, see
.BR otpme\-backup (1).
A number given here counts instead of the one in \fI.daily\fR, \fI.weekly\fR or \fI.monthly\fR.
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR names the snapshots it would delete.
.TP
.B gc
Remove the blocks no snapshot uses.
A pack file left empty is deleted, the others keep the space until \fBrepack\fR.
.TP
.B repack
Rewrite the pack files that hold blocks nobody uses, to get the space back.
.TP
.B compact
Compact the databases of the repository, to get back the space deleted entries took.
.TP
.B repair
Remove entries of the snapshot index that no snapshot has.
.TP
.B rebuild\-index
Build the index of the pack files again from the pack files, for an index that is lost or broken.
.hy
.ad
.SH EXAMPLES
.na
.nh
.TP
.B local-backup \-d /var/backups/berlin/share/data list
The snapshots of the share \fBdata\fR, on its backup server.
.TP
.B local-backup \-d /var/backups/berlin/share/data \-k \fIkey\fB verify 2026\-03\-29T02\-00\-05
Check that snapshot block by block.
.TP
.B local-backup \-d /var/backups/berlin/share/data retention \-\-daily 7 \-\-weekly 4 \-\-dry\-run
Which snapshots a retention of 7 days and 4 weeks would delete.
.TP
.B local-backup \-d /var/backups/berlin/share/data repack
Get back the space of deleted snapshots.
.hy
.ad
